   #@ (for Python hackers)
   This is the biggy.  Execute an _arbitrary_ python statement in
   the context of user.py's global variables.  If you use the #import 
   command or #@import, you can then do stuff like '#@mycode.func()', 
   etc.
   
   Since you have access to all of Lyntin's global variables, you can
   call any of Lyntin's functions too (although to use any of Lyntin's
   names from a file of your own code your file must import the proper
   Lyntin module).  A better method is to import the exported module
   which has hooks and functions which call the more important
   functions in the engine--such as 'lyntin_command'.

   Thus lyntin_command('flee') will send the text 'flee' through the
   Lyntin command interpreter.
   
   examples:
     #@for c in 'hello': print c
     #@PutMessage('this is Lyntin speaking')
     #@myvars.dead = 'yep'
     #@mycode.doit()

   see also: modules for more on building modules
